#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151943 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152155 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "39d79985-57fa-554a-b697-4bd6dd71cbe2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Basis of the Column Space of a Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "fc21ef44-25b7-5157-bbdb-c1284d1aa7ae" :question/math-academy-id "e-6254" :question/problem "Consider the matrix ![](images/fb/fb428b64d8d1fc020f343aea11f50ad92158ad4dcbf611b5decb44bd79ac221c.svg) Find a basis $\\mathcal B$ of $\\textrm{Col}(A).$" :question/worked-solution "In order to find a basis of $\\textrm{Col}(A),$ we need reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 2 & 5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -4 & 8\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to linearly independent vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the $1$st and $2$nd columns are pivot columns, while the $3$rd column is a non-pivot column.\n\nRemember that we need to pick the pivot columns from the *original matrix*, not from the reduced one. \n\nThe $1$st and $2$nd columns from the original matrix are $\\mathbf{a}_1$ and $\\mathbf{a}_2.$ So, a basis of $\\textrm{Col}(A)$ is $\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 \\},$ that is to say, \n\n![](images/7a/7abb144e511574809625c59d5ed6b7658983fc646bd0e6d6327f9f12356b8f08.svg)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151944 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152156 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "db27967e-f80c-587b-9b10-28f1a5518f57" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Given Set Is a Basis of the Column Space of a Matrix With Two Rows"}] :question/id #uuid "302059c1-d807-54d2-badc-a4a19b2f9c05" :question/math-academy-id "e-6255" :question/problem "The matrix $A$ is given below. Let $\\mathbf{a}_1$, $\\mathbf{a}_2$, and $\\mathbf{a}_3$ be the respective columns of $A.$ Determine if ![](images/b2/b22e2ffa6f6d88665e120e8dad82961e72f8f4a99484e8f81de311a66ab25988.svg) is a basis of $\\textrm{Col}(A).$ \nIf that is the case, find $x_1 \\cdot x_2$, where $\\mathbf{a}_3=x_1\\mathbf{a}_1+x_2\\mathbf{a}_2.$\n\n![](images/28/28f79983a195fa0152832aebd334cd8e1aaaa977dd9016347a3152403fd390c7.svg)" :question/worked-solution "First, we reduce the matrix $A$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 9 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 17\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 9 \\\\ 0 & -5 & -10\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=(-\\frac{1}{5}){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 9 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $2$nd columns. So, a basis of $\\textrm{Col}(A)$ is $\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2 \\},$ that is to say,\n\n![](images/45/4588c7d7ae4fd6cbc749373d83ab2d81e6a228f0cac03d5b959f4177f3f4cdf9.svg)\n\nNow, since the $3$rd column of the reduced matrix is a non-pivot column, then the entries in that column tell us the coefficients needed to express $\\mathbf{a}_3$ as a linear combination of all the other columns in the original matrix. So, we have\n\n![](images/fa/fa2ad666a6b80eac26bad12db322fa361578d94327e230d7c520c950add78923.svg)\n\nThis gives us $x_1 \\cdot x_2 = 5 \\cdot 2=10.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151945 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152157 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c3bf9ea3-d57f-53f6-aa3c-eddffe1a8cfa" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Given Set Is a Basis of the Column Space of a Matrix For a Larger Matrix"}] :question/id #uuid "d5bcbec9-0b72-5ba1-ad84-c770cf3a0c0b" :question/math-academy-id "e-6272" :question/problem "The matrix $A$ is given below. Let $\\mathbf{a}_1$, $\\mathbf{a}_2$,  $\\mathbf{a}_3$, and $\\mathbf{a}_4$ be the respective columns of $A.$ Determine if ![](images/af/afbd6ce90f2192c311c38ced9c1d9512c48bbf9a5e06236415ef9fe70883f9be.svg) is a basis of $\\textrm{Col}(A).$ If that is the case, find $x_1 \\cdot x_2$, where $\\mathbf{a}_4=x_1\\mathbf{a}_1+x_2\\mathbf{a}_2.$\n\n![](images/e2/e2ccba053acf8d050e305c3768c970ae635f4583163a867b6ef11f4e565ac1ae.svg)" :question/worked-solution "First, we reduce the matrix $A$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & -4 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -6 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -14 & -4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -6 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 2 & -14 & -4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -6 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -12 & -6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -6 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $2$nd columns. So, a basis of $\\textrm{Col}(A)$ is $\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2 \\},$ that is to say,\n![](images/6b/6b4306172b78c1e59bf66c8c08bf864aac2bcab65d4b1cc08ba4ddab0e5f1da3.svg)\n\nNow, since the $4$th column of the reduced matrix is a non-pivot column, then the entries in that column tell us the coefficients needed to express $\\mathbf{a}_4$ as a linear combination of all the other columns in the original matrix. So, we have\n\n![](images/b3/b3681d7f6c71d06ede32616ceec12ccd71da28373ee88eab451211af5a579212.svg)\n\nThis gives us $x_1 \\cdot x_2 = 2 \\cdot (-3) = -6.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435358 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152156 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "db27967e-f80c-587b-9b10-28f1a5518f57" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Given Set Is a Basis of the Column Space of a Matrix With Two Rows"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6b6ec014-b8b5-54e4-afed-992fa3dc7a1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435341} {:answer/id #uuid "6e1b53c1-ec77-5c04-b63c-840ca6b8f47c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435342} {:answer/id #uuid "c8fea29c-c9e0-5b67-ad2c-4468094390b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435347} {:answer/id #uuid "d5ca56d3-8b84-5db8-bf22-dfca5d0b2dc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435350} {:answer/id #uuid "fcbd9c6b-04ff-51f2-a5d9-53f32e994e70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\text{{}{w}_{1},{w}_{3}\\text{}}$ is not a basis of $\\text{Col}(W)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435352}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6b6ec014-b8b5-54e4-afed-992fa3dc7a1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435341} :answer-field/id #uuid "04e36a7f-df04-5cc4-ac8b-3bafab471a3b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435353}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fa6c549b-f344-52f9-9015-63f22befbecb" :question/math-academy-id "q-111501" :question/problem "The matrix $W$ is given below. Let ${w}_{1}$, ${w}_{2}$, and ${w}_{3}$ be the respective columns of $W.$\nDetermine if $B=\\text{{}{w}_{1},{w}_{3}\\text{}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(W).$ \nIf that is the case, find ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{3}$, where ${w}_{2}={x}_{1}{w}_{1}+{x}_{3}{w}_{3}.$\n\n$$\nW=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -9 & 6 \\\\ -5 & 15 & 10\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we reduce the matrix $W$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -9 & 6 \\\\ -5 & 15 & 10\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 2 \\\\ -5 & 15 & 10\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+5{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 20\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{20}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $3$rd columns. So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(W)$ is $\\{{w}_{1},{w}_{3}\\},$ that is to say,\n\n$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the $2$nd column of the reduced matrix is a non-pivot column, then the entries in that column tell us the coefficients needed to express ${w}_{2}$ as a linear combination of all the other columns in the original matrix. So, we have\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ 15\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{w}_{2}}={{(-3)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{1}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{w}_{1}}+{{(0)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{3}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{w}_{3}}.\n$$\n\nThis gives us ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{3}=(-3)\\cdot 0=0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247227 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152157 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c3bf9ea3-d57f-53f6-aa3c-eddffe1a8cfa" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Given Set Is a Basis of the Column Space of a Matrix For a Larger Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20472f46-7b35-5af4-bfc8-ec5caa02d79d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244599} {:answer/id #uuid "3984d68c-fbf8-5e5b-b17a-f60f741e298b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244823} {:answer/id #uuid "91dc3791-fbb0-58fc-8996-7caa1a749a72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245584} {:answer/id #uuid "a2ec493e-2751-54f6-8e50-a28c12be23aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245769} {:answer/id #uuid "e9271a88-7c05-5e7c-8fde-c4bd4934178e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\text{{}{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\text{}}$ is not a basis of $\\text{Col}(C)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246379}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a2ec493e-2751-54f6-8e50-a28c12be23aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245769} :answer-field/id #uuid "ece770f6-1100-5fc9-b657-fa1005c33ce5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246971}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a52283aa-8e43-5623-8f72-5c841a8ea5a0" :question/math-academy-id "q-111514" :question/problem "The matrix $C$ is given below. Let ${c}_{1}$, ${c}_{2}$,  ${c}_{3}$, and ${c}_{4}$ be the respective columns of $C.$ Determine if $B=\\text{{}{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\text{}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(C).$ If that is the case, find ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$, where ${c}_{4}={x}_{1}{c}_{1}+{x}_{2}{c}_{2}.$\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & -4 & -3 & -8 \\\\ 3 & -4 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we reduce the matrix $C$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & -4 & -3 & -8 \\\\ 3 & -4 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -4 & -4 & -12 \\\\ 3 & -4 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -4 & -4 & -12 \\\\ 0 & -4 & -4 & -12\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=(-\\frac{1}{4}){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -4 & -4 & -12\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+4{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $2$nd columns. So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(C)$ is ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2}},$ that is to say,\n\n$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the $4$th column of the reduced matrix is a non-pivot column, then the entries in that column tell us the coefficients needed to express ${c}_{4}$ as a linear combination of all the other columns in the original matrix. So, we have\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{c}_{4}}={{(4)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{1}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{c}_{1}}+{{(3)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{2}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{c}_{2}}.\n$$\n\nThis gives us ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=4\\cdot 3=12.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247228 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152157 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c3bf9ea3-d57f-53f6-aa3c-eddffe1a8cfa" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Given Set Is a Basis of the Column Space of a Matrix For a Larger Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00a32f43-637b-5461-9562-d1f4f4633f47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244311} {:answer/id #uuid "1fb27f68-618a-5048-b975-dfe5afb96846" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244593} {:answer/id #uuid "75239148-a2e0-57d6-8b72-443be29e2837" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245357} {:answer/id #uuid "ae6e1da8-792d-5297-a495-f7544f597c3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${{r}_{1},{r}_{2},{r}_{3}}$ is not a basis of $\\text{Col}(R)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245868} {:answer/id #uuid "e08eb7f2-6a8a-558b-bfcd-9fa051d938a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246312}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ae6e1da8-792d-5297-a495-f7544f597c3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${{r}_{1},{r}_{2},{r}_{3}}$ is not a basis of $\\text{Col}(R)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245868} :answer-field/id #uuid "5eb1ea7a-091d-5858-95cc-92d998b47922" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246715}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "840e6c59-db8d-561a-a36d-d9c509886863" :question/math-academy-id "q-111516" :question/problem "The matrix $R$ is given below. Let ${r}_{1}$, ${r}_{2}$,  ${r}_{3}$ and ${r}_{4}$ be the respective columns of $R.$\nDetermine if \n$B=\\text{{}{r}_{1},{r}_{2},{r}_{3}\\text{}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(R).$ \nIf that is the case, find ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}\\cdot {x}_{3}$, where ${r}_{4}={x}_{1}{r}_{1}+{x}_{2}{r}_{2}+{x}_{3}{r}_{3}.$ \n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 12 & -6 & 6 & -6\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we reduce the matrix $R$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 1 & -3 \\\\ 12 & -6 & 6 & -6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -2 & -6 \\\\ 12 & -6 & 6 & -6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-12){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -2 & -6 \\\\ 0 & -6 & -6 & -18\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -6 & -6 & -18\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+6{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $2$nd columns. So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(R)$ is ${{r}_{1},{r}_{2}}.$ \n\nTherefore, we conclude that $B={{r}_{1},{r}_{2},{r}_{3}}$ is not a basis of $\\text{Col}(R).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247233 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152157 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c3bf9ea3-d57f-53f6-aa3c-eddffe1a8cfa" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Given Set Is a Basis of the Column Space of a Matrix For a Larger Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "045d9074-4003-5b5e-b5db-6e1652c4adc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244349} {:answer/id #uuid "0bad38f8-0d9a-5b24-af08-76251c6d46d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244423} {:answer/id #uuid "63582946-97cd-5bc2-bfef-990af8f92316" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186245181} {:answer/id #uuid "ee28dbcf-222b-5f5f-a00a-f11ef5c76fd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${{s}_{1},{s}_{2},{s}_{3}}$ is not a basis of $\\text{Col}(S)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246417} {:answer/id #uuid "fbb0781b-ee06-51bd-a003-0819b4723c7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246518}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0bad38f8-0d9a-5b24-af08-76251c6d46d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186244423} :answer-field/id #uuid "3307ee7c-2109-56f3-bb59-ad4e80de5f2a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186246634}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7fa86926-dc53-59ca-bada-4e3c0c5873bf" :question/math-academy-id "q-111809" :question/problem "The matrix $S$ is given below. Let ${s}_{1}$, ${s}_{2}$,  ${s}_{3}$ and ${s}_{4}$ be the respective columns of $S.$\nDetermine if $B=\\text{{}{s}_{1},{s}_{2},{s}_{3}\\text{}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(S).$ \nIf that is the case, find ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}\\cdot {x}_{3}$, where ${s}_{4}={x}_{1}{s}_{1}+{x}_{2}{s}_{2}+{x}_{3}{s}_{3}.$ \n\n$$\nS=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -5 & 5 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 10 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 5 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we reduce the matrix $S$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -5 & 5 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 10 & -1 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 5 & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -5 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 & 9 \\\\ -1 & -2 & 5 & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -5 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 & 9 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 & 9\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=(-\\frac{1}{3}){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -5 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 0 & 9\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+3{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & -5 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $2$nd columns. So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(S)$ is ${{s}_{1},{s}_{2}}.$ \n\nTherefore, we conclude that $B={{s}_{1},{s}_{2},{s}_{3}}$ is not a basis of $\\text{Col}(S).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152579 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152156 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "db27967e-f80c-587b-9b10-28f1a5518f57" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Given Set Is a Basis of the Column Space of a Matrix With Two Rows"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a5750330-79d7-50e2-9ded-15a59101eba5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149982} {:answer/id #uuid "b785a5e7-ecc7-5455-a788-080e518def4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149983} {:answer/id #uuid "c9377f2f-9488-5514-aeeb-48e23c16df47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149984} {:answer/id #uuid "6fe9633c-3b72-5df8-adc7-1006f8331422" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149986} {:answer/id #uuid "ca8dfba9-883e-5552-a0f0-cb69cf694e80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\text{{}{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\text{}}$ is not a basis of $\\text{Col}(A)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423615}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6fe9633c-3b72-5df8-adc7-1006f8331422" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149986} :answer-field/id #uuid "60a2d415-9e9a-5c72-aafa-7f2b41592aa6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149981}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c555c8c9-de13-50dd-99c1-9c9975c94651" :question/math-academy-id "q-34462" :question/problem "The matrix $A$ is given below. Let ${a}_{1}$, ${a}_{2}$, and ${a}_{3}$ be the respective columns of $A.$ Determine if $B=\\text{{}{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\text{}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(A).$ If that is the case, find ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$, where ${a}_{3}={x}_{1}{a}_{1}+{x}_{2}{a}_{2}.$ \n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -6\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we reduce the matrix $A$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -6\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=-\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $2$nd columns. So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(A)$ is $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\},$ that is to say,\n\n$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the $3$rd column of the reduced matrix is a non-pivot column, then the entries in that column tell us the coefficients needed to express ${a}_{3}$ as a linear combination of all the other columns in the original matrix. So, we have\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{3}}={{(-1)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{1}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{1}}+{{(3)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{2}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{2}}.\n$$\n\nThis gives us ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=(-1)\\cdot 3=-3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152580 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152156 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "db27967e-f80c-587b-9b10-28f1a5518f57" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Given Set Is a Basis of the Column Space of a Matrix With Two Rows"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8a36f86a-9cf0-5a74-9e73-0b6b6057dd12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149988} {:answer/id #uuid "e8e7f094-f20a-53ac-ad68-c1b836150308" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149989} {:answer/id #uuid "f9334abb-558c-5342-a115-2acec5c72423" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${a_{1}, a_{3}}$ is not a basis of $Col(A)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149990} {:answer/id #uuid "15a8bf83-6bed-5f2d-a921-4c1c41806500" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149991} {:answer/id #uuid "39f0bda6-54c7-5b33-a8c3-6a85937caa55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149992}] :answer-field/id #uuid "2d955d5f-294e-53d0-813c-277787b98f85" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149987}] :question/id #uuid "40c35237-5ebd-5668-8d9f-8241b6761c15" :question/math-academy-id "q-34510" :question/problem "The matrix $A$ is given below. Let $a_{1}$, $a_{2}$, and $a_{3}$ are the respective columns of $A$. Determine if $B = {a_{1}, a_{3}}$ is a basis of $Col(A)$.\nIf that is the case, find $x_{1} \\cdot x_{3}$, where $a_{2} = x_{1}a_{1} + x_{3}a_{3}$.\n\n$A = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 3 & 4 \\\\ -2 & -6 & 5\\end{bmatrix}$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423620 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152155 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "39d79985-57fa-554a-b697-4bd6dd71cbe2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Basis of the Column Space of a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7ef60749-f0a3-5b92-b7ac-687d57c40bab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423608} {:answer/id #uuid "842c1b53-7a3c-5c3c-95cd-f635e53b9e76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423609} {:answer/id #uuid "8f1ff8c3-beef-5403-bf54-dedcfd80e6c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423610} {:answer/id #uuid "c1363c60-15a5-5bca-ae79-3b740d941738" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423613} {:answer/id #uuid "c62c345c-75b2-5956-a3af-45a35e4a85f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423614}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8f1ff8c3-beef-5403-bf54-dedcfd80e6c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423610} :answer-field/id #uuid "8d734853-a228-557f-9012-a5d01ef1cf52" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423618}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0c2bc939-4b4c-5161-9351-90818be5a0a1" :question/math-academy-id "q-75070" :question/problem "Given the matrix \n$V=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4\\end{aligned}].$ Find a basis $B$ of $\\text{Col}(V).$" :question/worked-solution "In order to find a basis of $\\text{Col}(V),$ we reduce the matrix $V$ to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & ↔{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to linearly independent vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the $1$st and $2$nd columns are pivot columns, while the $3$rd column is a non-pivot column.\n\nRemember that we need to pick the pivot columns from the *original matrix*, not from the reduced one. \n\nThe $1$st and $2$nd columns from the original matrix are ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}.$ So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(V)$ is $\\{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}\\},$ that is to say, \n\n$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345025 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152155 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "39d79985-57fa-554a-b697-4bd6dd71cbe2" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Basis of the Column Space of a Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f3c87ec-7d90-583e-b96b-afafaf2ca05e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342369} {:answer/id #uuid "91190a8c-43f8-548f-a084-7dd69417982a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 9 \\\\ -12\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343509} {:answer/id #uuid "a6d93a3b-f5c5-558d-96d8-0321e0803a10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343706} {:answer/id #uuid "aabffade-49e1-5d76-b5c2-5a0e200f247e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 9 \\\\ -12\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343741} {:answer/id #uuid "c7500b40-48ec-5263-869e-3e7f15e68d6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343986}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a6d93a3b-f5c5-558d-96d8-0321e0803a10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B={[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343706} :answer-field/id #uuid "c7cace19-2ac2-5d38-9b8e-8da19509dd92" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344840}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8e44b5dc-345c-58d4-a170-3fddfd0702d5" :question/math-academy-id "q-75073" :question/problem "Consider the matrix \n$W=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & 2 \\\\ -6 & 9 & -2 \\\\ 8 & -12 & 10\\end{aligned}].$ \nFind a basis $B$ of  $\\text{Col}(W).$" :question/worked-solution "In order to find a basis of $\\text{Col}(W),$ we reduce the matrix $W$ to row echelon form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & 2 \\\\ -6 & 9 & -2 \\\\ 8 & -12 & 10\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+3{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 8 & -12 & 10\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-\\frac{1}{2}){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot columns correspond to linearly independent vectors, while the non-pivot columns correspond to linearly dependent vectors that can be removed. Here, the $1$st and $3$rd columns are pivot columns, while the $2$nd column is a non-pivot column.\n\nRemember that we need to pick the pivot columns from the *original matrix*, not from the reduced one. \n\nThe $1$st and $3$rd columns from the original matrix are ${w}_{1}$ and ${w}_{3}.$ So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(W)$ is ${{w}_{1},{w}_{3}},$ that is to say, \n\n$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345139 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152156 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "db27967e-f80c-587b-9b10-28f1a5518f57" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Given Set Is a Basis of the Column Space of a Matrix With Two Rows"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "016024ef-c03f-5e15-8220-be9fb68e1bd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342260} {:answer/id #uuid "094fd71c-9a23-5d25-860d-bc340d895bbd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342315} {:answer/id #uuid "3e7a7b2d-982a-5570-b5f8-b8e34bf4d03c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342753} {:answer/id #uuid "6ed580a2-4268-5399-96f5-ea373b9218d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343193} {:answer/id #uuid "8255c71a-6cc1-58b7-8b63-f24165915a0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\text{{}{p}_{1},{p}_{2}\\text{}}$ is not a basis of $\\text{Col}(P)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343355}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3e7a7b2d-982a-5570-b5f8-b8e34bf4d03c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342753} :answer-field/id #uuid "3033d739-a4dc-5f2c-8494-cf265f068411" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344578}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "edfd11b7-178d-5b9f-89aa-f777ae4db963" :question/math-academy-id "q-75946" :question/problem "The matrix $P$ is given below. Let ${p}_{1}$, ${p}_{2}$, and ${p}_{3}$ be the respective columns of $P.$\nDetermine if $B=\\text{{}{p}_{1},{p}_{2}\\text{}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(P).$ \nIf that is the case, find ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$, where ${p}_{3}={x}_{1}{p}_{1}+{x}_{2}{p}_{2}.$\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we reduce the matrix $P$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-3){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $2$nd columns. So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(P)$ is ${{p}_{1},{p}_{2}},$ that is to say,\n\n$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the $3$rd column of the reduced matrix is a non-pivot column, then the entries in that column tell us the coefficients needed to express ${p}_{3}$ as a linear combination of all the other columns in the original matrix. So, we have\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{p}_{3}}={{(-2)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{1}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{p}_{1}}+{{(1)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{2}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{p}_{2}}.\n$$\n\nThis gives us ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=(-2)\\cdot 1=-2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345143 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152156 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "db27967e-f80c-587b-9b10-28f1a5518f57" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Given Set Is a Basis of the Column Space of a Matrix With Two Rows"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "32f287f2-7edb-54c9-83f8-d6a429304e1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342666} {:answer/id #uuid "54012245-c061-5945-8365-5fb96a1bba3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342937} {:answer/id #uuid "5b1e59c1-2c67-56da-a21f-63f2eaa8fa17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\text{{}{w}_{1},{w}_{3}\\text{}}$ is not a basis of $\\text{Col}(W)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342999} {:answer/id #uuid "9055039c-4784-5800-8ebe-48c648a6f6f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343499} {:answer/id #uuid "b2c372df-a7fc-55c4-aeb2-27fc2c39d58a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343808}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "54012245-c061-5945-8365-5fb96a1bba3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342937} :answer-field/id #uuid "9b4e5f9d-166d-57e6-b089-2301e828158d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344764}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8e38527f-7273-52c9-bec3-d94fc3af2837" :question/math-academy-id "q-75956" :question/problem "The matrix $W$ is given below. Let ${w}_{1}$, ${w}_{2}$, and ${w}_{3}$ be the respective columns of $W.$\nDetermine if $B=\\text{{}{w}_{1},{w}_{3}\\text{}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(W).$ \nIf that is the case, find ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{3}$, where ${w}_{2}={x}_{1}{w}_{1}+{x}_{3}{w}_{3}.$\n\n$$\nW=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -6 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -12 & 9\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we reduce the matrix $W$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -6 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -12 & 9\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 4 & -12 & 9\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $3$rd columns. So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(W)$ is $\\{{w}_{1},{w}_{3}\\},$ that is to say,\n\n$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]\\}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the $2$nd column of the reduced matrix is a non-pivot column, then the entries in that column tell us the coefficients needed to express ${w}_{2}$ as a linear combination of all the other columns in the original matrix. So, we have\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ -12\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{w}_{2}}={{(-3)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{1}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{w}_{1}}+{{(0)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{3}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{w}_{3}}.\n$$\n\nThis gives us ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{3}=(-3)\\cdot 0=0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186345149 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152157 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c3bf9ea3-d57f-53f6-aa3c-eddffe1a8cfa" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Given Set Is a Basis of the Column Space of a Matrix For a Larger Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3689c1b2-6595-5008-a7bc-92f44b77d2ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${{h}_{1},{h}_{2}}$ is not a basis of $\\text{Col}(H)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342687} {:answer/id #uuid "56326641-cf59-57eb-b78e-0a02a8992ad0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342955} {:answer/id #uuid "699ab4f9-6336-5399-980c-dc32fd2b7101" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343140} {:answer/id #uuid "961a3ec8-f1bb-5d87-a172-a8257890a9cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343551} {:answer/id #uuid "e3d6beb2-7ff7-5c87-91be-6fa2a12c62ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344239}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3689c1b2-6595-5008-a7bc-92f44b77d2ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "${{h}_{1},{h}_{2}}$ is not a basis of $\\text{Col}(H)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342687} :answer-field/id #uuid "9df7a474-f23c-56da-ad81-c35426f28ffc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344768}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e2ee7dc8-6fbf-5fd7-b2a6-20aab9680dc7" :question/math-academy-id "q-75977" :question/problem "The matrix $H$ is given below. Let ${h}_{1}$, ${h}_{2}$,  ${h}_{3}$ and ${h}_{4}$ be the respective columns of $H.$\nDetermine if $B=\\text{{}{h}_{1},{h}_{2}\\text{}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(H).$ \nIf that is the case, find ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$, where ${h}_{4}={x}_{1}{h}_{1}+{x}_{2}{h}_{2}.$ \n\n$$\nH=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & 12 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 9 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we reduce the matrix $H$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}H & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 & 12 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 9 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 6 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 9 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 6 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{3} & :=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 6 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st, $2$nd, and $3$rd columns. So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(H)$ is ${{h}_{1},{h}_{2},{h}_{3}}.$ \n\nTherefore, we conclude that $B={{h}_{1},{h}_{2}}$ is not a basis of $\\text{Col}(H).$"}]}
